---
import { Image } from 'astro:assets'

import annexDark from '@/assets/projects/annex-dark.png'
import annexLight from '@/assets/projects/annex-light.png'
import RouteFoot from '@/components/site/case-study/route-foot.astro'
import RouteBar from '@/components/site/nav/route-bar.astro'
import SectionNav from '@/components/site/section-nav/section-nav.astro'
import BaseLayout from '@/layouts/base.astro'
import { getLinkTargetAttributes } from '@/lib/links'

const artifactLinks = [
  { label: 'Recorded demo', href: 'https://annex.erclx.dev' },
  { label: 'GitHub', href: 'https://github.com/erclx/annex' },
]

// The route's opening leads the rail, per the note in `jobtriage.astro`.
const navItems = [
  { id: 'annex', label: 'annex' },
  { id: 'what', label: 'what' },
  { id: 'question', label: 'question' },
  { id: 'findings', label: 'findings' },
  { id: 'graph', label: 'graph' },
  { id: 'refusal', label: 'refusal' },
  { id: 'freshness', label: 'freshness' },
  { id: 'limits', label: 'limits' },
]

const evaluation = [
  {
    arm: 'Whole Act in the prompt',
    recallOriginal: '1.00',
    recallAmended: '1.00',
    promptTokens: '1 416 797',
  },
  {
    arm: 'Search only',
    recallOriginal: '0.61',
    recallAmended: '0.71',
    promptTokens: '51 622',
  },
  {
    arm: 'Search plus reference traversal',
    recallOriginal: '0.83',
    recallAmended: '0.81',
    promptTokens: '193 575',
  },
]

// The alt text is the annex README's own for the same two files.
const heroAlt =
  'The Annex landing page: a description box and twelve recorded questions'

const heroImageClass = 'block h-auto w-full'
const headingClass =
  'text-foreground font-display text-2xl leading-tight font-semibold md:text-3xl'
---

<BaseLayout
  title="annex · Eric Le"
  description="An EU AI Act compliance agent that maps a described system to cited articles, and tests whether retrieval helps when the whole law fits in context."
  surface="case-study"
>
  <SectionNav items={navItems} instant />

  <RouteBar here="annex" />

  <main>
    <section id="annex" class="page-inset pt-16 pb-12 md:pt-20 md:pb-16">
      <div class="mx-auto prose-column">
        <p data-fade class="text-muted-foreground text-label tracking-wide uppercase">
          Project
        </p>
        <h1
          data-fade
          class="font-display text-foreground mt-4 text-[2.25rem] leading-tight font-semibold md:text-[2.75rem]"
        >
          annex
        </h1>
        <p data-fade class="text-muted-foreground text-lede mt-4">
          An EU AI Act compliance agent that maps a described system to cited
          articles, and tests whether retrieval helps when the whole law fits in
          context.
        </p>
        <ul data-fade class="mt-6 flex flex-wrap gap-x-6">
          {
            artifactLinks.map((link) => (
              <li>
                <a
                  href={link.href}
                  {...getLinkTargetAttributes(link.href)}
                  class="text-foreground text-label inline-flex min-h-11 min-w-11 items-center underline-offset-4 hover:underline"
                >
                  {link.label}
                </a>
              </li>
            ))
          }
        </ul>

        {/* The landing page the annex README opens on. The two themes are two
            files, so each shows under its own theme. */}
        <figure
          data-fade
          class="figure-breakout bg-card border-border/60 mt-12 overflow-hidden rounded-lg border"
        >
          <Image
            src={annexLight}
            alt={heroAlt}
            class={`${heroImageClass} dark:hidden`}
          />
          <Image
            src={annexDark}
            alt={heroAlt}
            class={`${heroImageClass} hidden dark:block`}
          />
        </figure>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="what" class="page-inset py-16 md:py-20">
      <div class="mx-auto prose-column">
        <h2 data-fade class={headingClass}>what</h2>
        <p data-fade class="text-foreground text-lede mt-6">
          A user describes an AI system. The agent returns the articles that may
          attach, cited to the text it read them from, and says so plainly when
          the law does not settle the question.
        </p>
        <p data-fade class="text-muted-foreground text-body mt-6">
          It does not determine whether a system complies. It finds the text a
          person needs to read.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="question" class="page-inset py-16 md:py-20">
      <div class="mx-auto prose-column">
        <h2 data-fade class={headingClass}>question</h2>
        <p data-fade class="text-foreground text-lede mt-6">
          I wanted to know whether retrieval still helped when a model could read
          the whole EU AI Act.
        </p>
        <p data-fade class="text-muted-foreground text-body mt-6">
          The Act runs to roughly 90,000 words, which fits inside a large
          context window. That made the usual answer stop being obvious: if a
          model can read the whole law, is retrieval still worth building?
        </p>
        <p data-fade class="text-muted-foreground text-body mt-6">
          So rather than assume a vector database belonged in the design, I
          compared three arms on the same twelve questions. The whole Act in the
          context window. Vector search alone. Vector search followed by
          traversal of a graph built from the Act's own cross-references.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="findings" class="page-inset py-16 md:py-20">
      <div class="mx-auto prose-column">
        <h2 data-fade class={headingClass}>findings</h2>
        <p data-fade class="text-foreground text-lede mt-6">
          In this twelve-question evaluation, full-context reading had the
          highest recall.
        </p>
        <p data-fade class="text-muted-foreground text-body mt-6">
          The conclusion is narrower than retrieval not working. Retrieval is not
          automatically the right answer when the complete, trusted source fits
          in context.
        </p>

        {/* Lifted from the annex README's evaluation table, which the career
            source does not carry yet, with its own sentence as the caption. */}
        <figure
          data-fade
          class="figure-breakout bg-card mt-10 overflow-x-auto rounded-lg p-6 md:p-10"
        >
          <figcaption class="text-muted-foreground font-mono text-label mb-4">
            The same twelve questions ran three ways on one RTX 5090, 72 runs in
            all.
          </figcaption>
          <table class="font-mono text-label w-full min-w-[32rem]">
            <thead>
              <tr class="text-muted-foreground">
                <th class="text-left font-normal">Arm</th>
                <th class="text-right font-normal tabular-nums">
                  Recall, original
                </th>
                <th class="text-right font-normal tabular-nums">
                  Recall, amended
                </th>
                <th class="text-right font-normal tabular-nums">
                  Prompt tokens, original
                </th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody class="text-foreground">
              {
                evaluation.map((row) => (
                  <tr>
                    <td class="py-1 pr-4">{row.arm}</td>
                    <td class="py-1 pl-4 text-right tabular-nums">
                      {row.recallOriginal}
                    </td>
                    <td class="py-1 pl-4 text-right tabular-nums">
                      {row.recallAmended}
                    </td>
                    <td class="py-1 pl-4 text-right tabular-nums">
                      {row.promptTokens}
                    </td>
                  </tr>
                ))
              }
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="graph" class="page-inset py-16 md:py-20">
      <div class="mx-auto prose-column">
        <h2 data-fade class={headingClass}>graph</h2>
        <p data-fade class="text-foreground text-lede mt-6">
          The Act cites itself in ordinary sentences, phrases like "in accordance
          with Article 43" sitting inside running prose. Those citations are
          parsed into several hundred graph edges programmatically rather than
          inferred by a model.
        </p>
        <p data-fade class="text-muted-foreground text-body mt-6">
          That is what makes a multi-hop question answerable at all. Asking what
          follows from a system being high-risk walks <code class="font-mono"
            >Article 6</code
          > to <code class="font-mono">Annex III</code> to <code class="font-mono"
            >Articles 8-15</code
          >, instead of hoping one retrieved passage happens to hold the whole
          chain.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="refusal" class="page-inset py-16 md:py-20">
      <div class="mx-auto prose-column">
        <h2 data-fade class={headingClass}>refusal</h2>
        <p data-fade class="text-foreground text-lede mt-6">
          The refusal is a branch in the pipeline rather than error handling.
          Where the retrieved and traversed text does not settle a question, the
          agent says so instead of extrapolating.
        </p>
        <p data-fade class="text-muted-foreground text-body mt-6">
          The clearest case is a threshold regulators have not defined. Whether a
          later modification to an already-deployed system counts as substantial
          enough to trigger full high-risk compliance is not settled by the text,
          so the agent declines to settle it either.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="freshness" class="page-inset py-16 md:py-20">
      <div class="mx-auto prose-column">
        <h2 data-fade class={headingClass}>freshness</h2>
        <p data-fade class="text-foreground text-lede mt-6">
          A Digital Omnibus amendment moved the deadline for standalone high-risk
          obligations from 2 August 2026 to 2 December 2027, and it took effect
          while the project was in progress.
        </p>
        <p data-fade class="text-muted-foreground text-body mt-6">
          Querying the unamended corpus returns a confidently wrong deadline.
          Querying the amended corpus returns the right one, with the citation
          change visible between them. That is a better argument for keeping a
          corpus current than any sentence about it.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="limits" class="page-inset py-16 md:py-20">
      <div class="mx-auto prose-column">
        <h2 data-fade class={headingClass}>limits</h2>
        <p data-fade class="text-foreground text-lede mt-6">
          It names obligations and cites text. The compliance judgment stays with
          the reader.
        </p>
        <p data-fade class="text-muted-foreground text-body mt-6">
          The deployed page replays twelve recorded answers rather than calling a
          model. The generation model needs 30GB of GPU memory and nothing free
          hosts that, so asking a new question means running the pipeline
          locally.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>

  <RouteFoot />

  <script>
    import { initWayHome } from '@/components/site/case-study/way-home'
    import { initReveal } from '@/lib/reveal'

    initReveal()
    initWayHome()
  </script>
</BaseLayout>
